---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="What Decks Really Cost in Maryland (2026) | Belvedere Decks"
  description="Real 2026 deck pricing: new composite boards starting at $8,000, boards plus railings starting at $12,000, new custom decks starting at $17,500. What moves the number and what a cheap bid leaves out."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Published pricing for 2026</p>
      <h1>What decks really cost.</h1>
      <div class="rule"></div>
      <p class="lead">
        Most deck companies won't put prices in writing until they've sat in your
        kitchen. Here's what projects actually run in Maryland in 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <table class="stack">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th><a href="/services/deck-resurfacing">Deck Resurfacing: boards only</a></th>
            <td>Every deck board replaced with premium composite. Your frame and railings stay. <strong>Starting at $8,000.</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th><a href="/services/deck-resurfacing">Deck Resurfacing: boards + railings</a></th>
            <td>Everything you see and touch is new: boards, railings, and edge trim on your existing frame. <strong>Starting at $12,000.</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th><a href="/services/new-deck-builds">New Deck Builds</a></th>
            <td>A fully custom new deck: designed, engineered, permitted, and inspected. Includes tear-out of an old deck where needed. <strong>Starting at $17,500.</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Our projects start at $6,000. That is not a minimum we invented to sound
        exclusive. A crew day costs the same whether your deck is 80 square feet or
        200, so below a certain size the price stops falling. If you have a small
        platform and a set of steps, call anyway. We will tell you the number, and you
        can decide.
      </p>

      <h2>What moves the number</h2>
      <p>
        Size. Board line: we install TimberTech and Trex, from entry to architectural
        grade. Railing choice. Stairs and lighting. Height off the ground. And the one
        nobody mentions: the condition of the frame underneath. That's why we inspect
        before we quote, and why joist replacement has a written allowance in your
        contract instead of becoming a surprise charge later.
      </p>

      <h2>What should make you suspicious</h2>
      <p>
        A bid thousands below everyone else usually means no permit, no waterproof
        flashing where the deck meets the house, support boards spaced too far apart for
        composite decking, or railings screwed to the boards instead of bolted to the
        frame. Those are exactly the things you can't see at the barbecue, and pay for
        in year five.
      </p>

      <div class="notice">
        <strong>Every price we quote includes:</strong> permits and inspections where
        required, haul-away of all debris, and a 10-year written workmanship warranty on
        top of the 25 to 50 year manufacturer board warranties. <strong>On a new build, that
        also means the structure done right:</strong> footings dug to code depth, full
        waterproof flashing where the deck meets the house, and steel connectors with
        every fastener hole filled.
      </div>

      <h2>How our pricing compares</h2>
      <p>
        High-end design firms in this market start their composite projects around
        $30,000. Budget operators advertise numbers that quietly leave out the
        structure. We land in between on purpose: premium specification at an honest
        number, with the math shown.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 24px;"><a class="btn primary" href="/estimate">Get a Free Estimate</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
